        <h3 class="text-center mb-5">What is a finite-state automaton?</h3>
        <p>
            A finite-state automaton is a dynamic, discrete, invariant system in which the sets of input,
            output and state are finite.<br>
            The system can be in any of a finite number of internal states that define
            the automaton. A particularly important class of automata is that of automata capable of recognizing whether
            or not
            a string is part of a given language or not: recognizer automata.<br>
            Such a finite-state automaton consists of a finite set of states and a finite set of
            transitions from one state to another. For each distinct pair formed by a state of the automaton (the
            departure state) and an alphabet symbol, there is a transition to an arrival state. The symbol of the
            pair is said to be associated with the transition, or even that the transition "accepts the symbol." The
            state of
            arrival state can also coincide with the start state. Each finite-state automaton is uniquely associated
            with
            an oriented graph called a transition diagram. The nodes of the graph coincide with the states of the
            automaton.
            If there is a transition from state q to state p with a as the associated symbol, then there is an arc
            oriented from node q to node p and labeled with the symbol a.<br>
            A mathematical model formed by the quintuple is needed to describe an automaton:<br>
            A = {S, I, U, f, g}<br>
            where:
        <ul>
            <li>S is the finite set of states</li>
            <li>I is the finite set of possible input symbols</li>
            <li>U is the finite set of possible output symbols</li>
            <li>f is the transition function of successive internal states, which connects the state at the instant
                next to the current value at the input and the current state</li>
            <li>g is the function that determines the value of the outputs</li>
        </ul>
        </p>
